-- title:
--   Isomorphisms of perfect closures whose Galois pullbacks differ by an $\ell$-adic unit are Frobenius-equivalent
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a prime and let $k$, $l$ be algebraically closed fields. Let $K$ be a field extension of $k$ that is essentially of finite type over $k$, contains an element transcendental over $k$, and satisfies $\ell\neq0$ in $K$; let $L$ be any field extension of $l$. For a field $F$ fix an algebraic closure $\bar F$, let $F^{\mathrm{perf}}\subseteq\bar F$ be its perfect closure and $G_F=\operatorname{Aut}_F(\bar F)$ its absolute Galois group, let $\mathcal G_F(\ell)$ be the maximal pro-$\ell$ quotient of $G_F$, let $\Pi^a_\ell(F)$ be the quotient of $\mathcal G_F(\ell)$ by the closure of its commutator subgroup, and let $\pi_F:G_F\to\Pi^a_\ell(F)$ be the projection. For $u\in\mathbb Z_\ell$ let $u_n\in\{0,\dots,\ell^n-1\}$ be its residue modulo $\ell^n$. The theorem involves the following notions.
--
--   - *Field side.* $\mathrm{Isom}_{k,l}(K^{\mathrm{perf}},L^{\mathrm{perf}})$ is the set of ring isomorphisms $\alpha:K^{\mathrm{perf}}\to L^{\mathrm{perf}}$ mapping the image of $k$ onto the image of $l$. An element $\beta$ of this set is a *Frobenius step* from $\alpha$ if there is a prime $p$ with $p=0$ in $L$ and $\beta(x)=\alpha(x)^p$ for all $x\in K^{\mathrm{perf}}$; write $\alpha\sim_{\mathrm{Fr}}\beta$ for the equivalence relation generated by Frobenius steps.
--   - *Galois side.* For a field $F$ let $\Pi^c_\ell(F)$ be the quotient of $\mathcal G=\mathcal G_F(\ell)$ by the closure of $[\overline{[\mathcal G,\mathcal G]},\mathcal G]$, let $\Delta_\ell(F)$ be the kernel of the projection $\Pi^c_\ell(F)\to\Pi^a_\ell(F)$, and for $x,y\in\Pi^a_\ell(F)$ let $[x,y]\in\Delta_\ell(F)$ be the image of the commutator of lifts of $x$ and $y$ to $\mathcal G$. $\mathrm{Isom}^c_\ell(K,L)$ is the set of topological group isomorphisms $\varphi:\Pi^a_\ell(L)\to\Pi^a_\ell(K)$ that are bracket-compatible, i.e. for which some topological group isomorphism $\psi:\Delta_\ell(L)\to\Delta_\ell(K)$ satisfies $\psi([x,y])=[\varphi(x),\varphi(y)]$ for all $x,y$. An element $\chi$ of this set is a *scalar step* from $\varphi$ if there is $u\in\mathbb Z_\ell^\times$ with $\varphi(x)^{u_n}\to\chi(x)$ as $n\to\infty$ for every $x\in\Pi^a_\ell(L)$.
--   - *Graph.* $\varphi\in\mathrm{Isom}^c_\ell(K,L)$ is a *Galois pullback* of $\alpha$ if there is a ring isomorphism $\tilde\alpha:\bar K\to\bar L$ extending $\alpha$ such that for every $\sigma\in G_L$ there is $\tau\in G_K$ with $\tilde\alpha\circ\tau=\sigma\circ\tilde\alpha$ on $\bar K$ and $\varphi(\pi_L(\sigma))=\pi_K(\tau)$.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $\alpha,\beta\in\mathrm{Isom}_{k,l}(K^{\mathrm{perf}},L^{\mathrm{perf}})$ and $\varphi,\chi\in\mathrm{Isom}^c_\ell(K,L)$. If $\varphi$ is a Galois pullback of $\alpha$, $\chi$ is a Galois pullback of $\beta$, and $\chi$ is a scalar step from $\varphi$, then
--
--   $$
--   \alpha\sim_{\mathrm{Fr}}\beta .
--   $$
--
--   This is injectivity at the level of representatives: two isomorphisms of perfect closures whose Galois pullbacks differ by an $\ell$-adic unit agree up to Frobenius twists. Passing to classes (field isomorphisms modulo Frobenius steps, Galois isomorphisms modulo scalar steps), it shows that a class of Galois isomorphisms is the pullback of at most one class of field isomorphisms.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The two sets are `PerfectIsom k K l L` and `IsomC ℓ K L`; the hypotheses are `PullbackGraph α φ`, `PullbackGraph β χ` and `ScalarStep φ χ`, and the conclusion `Relation.EqvGen FrobeniusStep α β` says that $\alpha$ and $\beta$ have the same class in `IsomF k K l L`. Only $\ell\neq0$ in $K$ is assumed, and no finiteness hypothesis is placed on $L$.
